> **"I've set dozens of 2ct lab marquise stones in the last two years. The most common mistake I see is skipping the 360° video and buying on grade alone. At 2ct, the face-up size exposes the cut quality more than at 1ct. A D-VVS1 with a harsh bow-tie looks worse than an E-VVS2 with a clean belly. Always watch the video. The certificate tells you the grade — the video tells you the stone."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

**Verdict:** Start at the $2,212 E-VVS1. Watch the 360° video on the retailer listing and check the L:W ratio — target 1.85–2.05 for a 2ct marquise. If L:W is better on the $2,232 stone, the $20 is worth it.

## VVS1 Tier — IGI 2ct D-VVS1 ($2,403)

The IGI D-VVS1 Ideal at $2,403 is the best-documented 2ct IGI lab marquise in certified inventory.

**What changes from entry to VVS1:** One colour grade. The entry stone at $2,212 is already VVS1 clarity. The step from $2,212 to $2,403 buys D colour only — E→D, $191 for one colour grade. That is the only change.

D colour is the absolute maximum on the [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) colour scale. E is one grade below. In a setting, on a finger, D and E look the same — no normal viewer can tell them apart. The D on the certificate is the strongest possible colour documentation. At $191 more, it is one of the cheapest D upgrades in certified inventory.

**When to choose D-VVS1 at $2,403:** You want the absolute maximum IGI colour grade. You're setting in platinum and want D + VVS1 on the certificate. You plan to pass the stone down and want the strongest documentation. You have the $191 and the D grade gives you satisfaction.

