**Verdict:** D-VVS1 at $2,403 is the rational ceiling for visual quality. D-IF/D-FL at $4,409 is the rational choice only for documentation. The $2,006 step adds a certificate grade, not a better-looking stone. Save it for the setting — at 2ct, the setting investment matters as much as the stone grade.

## Setting Pairing for 2ct Lab Grown Marquise

A 2ct lab marquise measures approximately 14x7mm face-up. It is a substantial stone. The elongation is dramatic — longer and narrower than a 2ct oval. The tips carry more weight and are more exposed than at 1ct.

**Six-prong solitaire — standard recommendation.** Prongs at each tip and two on each side give full protection without covering the belly. A six-prong solitaire from $1,355 in white gold keeps the total ring under $3,800 with the E-VVS1 entry stone. See our [marquise solitaire engagement ring guide](/marquise-solitaire-engagement-ring).

**Bezel for daily wear.** At 2ct, the marquise tips have more surface area and more exposure. A full bezel wraps both tips completely. Total ring with E-VVS1 entry at $2,212: under $3,500. Trade-off: the metal lip reduces side brilliance slightly. See our [marquise bezel engagement ring guide](/marquise-bezel-engagement-ring).

**Pavé for visual impact.** A 2ct marquise at centre with pavé shoulders reads significantly larger on the finger. The elongation combined with pavé creates the largest visual footprint in the engagement ring market at this budget. Total ring with GCAL D-VVS1: under $5,000.

**Metal pairing:** D and E colour lab stones look best in platinum or white gold. Cool metal amplifies the colourless appearance. At 2ct, platinum is worth considering for durability given the tip exposure. See our [platinum marquise engagement ring guide](/platinum-marquise-engagement-ring) and [white gold marquise engagement ring guide](/white-gold-marquise-engagement-ring).

