## Pros and Cons — 2ct Lab Grown Marquise Diamond

**Pros:**
- **72–85% savings vs natural** at every grade tier from E-VVS1 to D-VVS1.
- **Fractional arbitrage** — 2.12ct E-VS1 at $1,890, more carat weight at a lower price than 2.00ct entry.
- **Complete rings under $3,600** — $2,212 stone + $1,250 bezel setting = $3,462 total.
- **GCAL available at 2ct** — the 8X performance guarantee is accessible at $2,883 for D-VVS1.
- **Face-up advantage** — 2ct marquise measures ~14x7mm. The elongation makes it one of the largest face-up options in this price range.
- **Same physical properties as natural** — 10 Mohs hardness, identical refractive index, identical brilliance.

**Cons:**
- **No resale value** — lab prices continue to decline. A $2,212 lab stone today may be worth $300–$400 on resale in five years.
- **Bow-tie risk amplified at 2ct** — the longer belly section makes a poorly proportioned marquise more visually obvious. Always watch the 360° video. See our [marquise diamond bow-tie guide](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie).
- **Tip protection is critical at this size** — at 14x7mm, the pointed tips are longer and more exposed than at 1ct. Bezel or six-prong settings are non-negotiable for daily wear.
- **Fractional arbitrage stones are VS1/VS2 only** — if you want VVS clarity with the fractional size advantage, it does not exist in current inventory. The VVS stones are priced at the 2.00ct tier.
- **Certificate verification required** — verify IGI cert at [igi.org](https://www.igi.org/) and GCAL cert at [gcalusa.com](https://www.gcalusa.com/) before purchase.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### How much does a 2 carat lab grown marquise diamond cost?

**$1,734 to $5,291** in current certified inventory. The core range for 2.00ct stones is $2,212 (IGI E-VVS1 entry) to $2,883 (GCAL D-VVS1). Fractional arbitrage picks — 2.10–2.22ct stones — start at $1,734. Above $4,000, you reach IF and FL grades. A natural 2ct G-VS2 marquise costs ~$8,000; natural D-VVS1 is $15,000+.

### What is the 7x natural premium for 2ct marquise diamonds?

**Natural D-VVS1 2ct marquise costs ~$15,000. Lab D-VVS1 costs $2,403. That is 6.2x more for the natural.** Lab entry at $2,212 vs natural D-VVS1 at $15,000 is 6.8x — the 7x headline figure. The premium is structural: natural 2ct stones hit a rarity price spike that lab production does not face.

### What is the Fractional Size Arbitrage at 2ct?

**It is the price advantage of buying a 2.10–2.22ct stone over a precisely 2.00ct stone.** Non-round weights avoid the 2ct pricing threshold. A 2.12ct E-VS1 costs $1,890 — $342 less than a 2.00ct E-VVS2 at $2,232, with 0.12ct more weight. Trade-off: VS1 vs VVS2 clarity (both eye-clean). Best arbitrage pick: [IGI 2.12ct E-VS1 at $1,890](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29990354?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

### IGI or GCAL for a 2ct lab marquise?

**IGI if you want the lowest price. GCAL if you want the 8X grade guarantee.** IGI D-VVS1 costs $2,403. GCAL D-VVS1 costs $2,883 — $480 more. The stones look identical. GCAL's 8X report guarantees all 8 performance grades. IGI issues a grade statement without that guarantee. Choose GCAL if the written guarantee matters for documentation or resale.

### What colour grade should I choose for a 2ct lab marquise?

**E is the sweet spot in white gold or platinum. G in yellow or rose gold.** D adds $191 over E for a colour difference invisible under normal conditions. In cool metal, E and D look the same. In yellow or rose gold, the warm setting makes E-D colour redundant — step down to G and save $150–$200 vs E. See our [marquise diamond colour guide](/marquise-diamond-color-guide).

### What clarity grade is enough at 2ct?

**VVS1 is the practical ceiling. VS1 is eye-clean and still strong.** At 2ct, VS1 inclusions are not visible to the naked eye. VVS1 has a cleaner certificate. IF and FL add $2,000 over D-VVS1 with zero visual return. Choose VVS1 if you want the cleanest cert under $3,000. Choose VS1 (via fractional arbitrage picks) if you want maximum value. See our [marquise diamond clarity guide](/marquise-diamond-clarity-guide).

### Does a 2ct lab marquise have a bow-tie?

**It can — and bow-tie is more visible at 2ct than at 1ct.** The longer belly section of a 2ct marquise (14x7mm) makes a dark shadow across the centre more noticeable. Select IGI Excellent or Ideal cut grades to filter the worst cases. Always watch the 360° video on the retailer listing before purchasing. See our [marquise diamond bow-tie guide](/marquise-diamond-bow-tie).

### What L:W ratio should I target for a 2ct lab marquise?

**1.85–2.05.** At 2ct, the slightly wider range accommodates the larger absolute dimensions. Below 1.80, the stone reads wide and stubby. Above 2.10, tip fragility increases and the bow-tie risk rises. Compare multiple stones at the same price and choose the one closest to your preferred L:W. See our [marquise diamond length to width ratio guide](/marquise-diamond-length-to-width-ratio).

### What setting is best for a 2ct lab marquise?

**Six-prong solitaire or bezel — tip protection is more critical at 2ct.** The 14x7mm tips are longer and more exposed than at 1ct. A bezel fully wraps both tips for maximum daily-wear protection. A six-prong solitaire with prongs at each tip is the standard recommendation for visibility plus protection. See our [marquise solitaire engagement ring guide](/marquise-solitaire-engagement-ring).

### What metal should I pair with a 2ct D-E lab marquise?

**Platinum or white gold.** D and E colour stones look brightest in cool metal — the white setting amplifies the colourless appearance. At 2ct, platinum is worth considering over white gold for its durability (no replating needed). See our [platinum marquise engagement ring guide](/platinum-marquise-engagement-ring) for the full setting selection.

### Is a 2ct lab marquise bigger than a 2ct round?

**Yes — significantly.** A 2ct marquise measures ~14x7mm face-up vs ~8.1mm for a 2ct round. The elongated shape gives the marquise 15–20% more face-up area per carat than a round. The finger-slimming effect is more dramatic at 2ct than at 1ct. See our [oval vs marquise diamond](/oval-vs-marquise-diamond) shape comparison.

### Does a 2ct lab marquise hold its value?

**No.** Lab diamond prices have declined significantly since 2021 and continue to fall as production scales. A $2,212 lab stone today may be worth $300–$500 on resale. Natural 2ct marquise holds value better — and even natural engagement rings typically sell at a loss. Buy lab at 2ct because you want to wear it, not because you expect to sell it.

### How many 2ct lab marquise stones are available?

The dataset covers 22 true 2.00ct IGI and GCAL stones from $2,212 to $5,291, plus 17 fractional 2.10–2.22ct stones from $1,734 to $2,164. The [full certified lab marquise inventory](https://www.bluenile.com/lab-diamonds/marquise-cut-diamonds?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) covers 313 total stones. Filter by carat to isolate the 2ct tier.

### Can I get a complete 2ct lab marquise engagement ring for under $4,000?

**Yes.** The cheapest complete ring is $3,462 — E-VVS1 at $2,212 + Comfort Fit Bezel at $1,250. A six-prong solitaire combination runs $3,567. Even the GCAL D-VVS1 in a platinum four-prong solitaire is $4,063. Natural 2ct equivalent rings start at $9,500+.

### Is a 2ct lab marquise a good engagement ring?

**Yes — it is an outstanding value.** At $2,212–$2,883 for a certified D-E colour, VVS1 stone, a 2ct lab marquise delivers a large, brilliant diamond for well under $3,000. The complete ring stays under $4,000. Natural 2ct equivalent rings start at three times that price. See the [lab grown marquise diamond guide](/lab-grown-marquise-diamond) for the full size comparison across all carats.

### Why is a 2.12ct lab marquise cheaper than a 2.00ct?

**Non-round carat weights avoid the psychological pricing spike at round thresholds.** At exactly 2.00ct, the price per carat jumps because the round number carries marketing premium. At 2.12ct, the stone weighs more but the per-carat price is lower. The result: more diamond for less money. The trade-off is VS1 vs VVS2 clarity — both eye-clean, but different certificate grades.

